INT. NIGHT FERRY - PASSENGER CABIN - NIGHT

Fog at the portholes. A sodium lamp swings on its chain. Plastic seats bolted in rows. The engine thrums.

CORA WHITLOCK (30s) sits clutching a paper cup. Across from her, LEO BASSETT (30s) tries to sleep with his coat over his face.

A figure in the far aisle. Tall. Wet. Unmoving.

Cora notices him. Lowers her cup.

He steps closer. Water drips from his sleeves onto the deck.

                    STRANGER
          This seat taken?

                    CORA
          Plenty of seats.

                    STRANGER
          I like this one.

He sits. Right beside her. Too close. She shifts away. He watches her do it.

Leo lifts the coat. Squints at him. Sinks back down.

                    STRANGER (CONT'D)
          Is he your husband?

                    CORA
          Brother.

                    STRANGER
          Pity.

She stands. He grabs her wrist. Cold. Wet. She yanks free.

                    CORA
          Leo!

Leo bolts upright. The coat falls. The stranger smiles at him.

                    STRANGER
          Easy, friend.

Leo steps between them. Shoves him back. The man laughs, a bright, delighted bark, and raises both hands.

                    STRANGER (CONT'D)
          Oswin. My name is Oswin.

                    CORA
          I don't care.

                    OSWIN
          You will.

He reaches into his coat. Leo tenses. Oswin pulls out a ticket stub, damp, torn. Holds it up in the lamplight.

                    OSWIN (CONT'D)
          Last ferry to Harrow Point. Nineteen fifty-one.

Leo takes it. Reads it. His face loses color.

                    LEO
          That's a prop.

                    OSWIN
          Is it?

Cora backs away. Her heel hits the bulkhead. Oswin rises slowly. Water pools around his shoes.

He steps toward her. She screams.

The lamp swings. In the swing, he is gone. The seat is empty and wet.

Leo drops the ticket. It lands in a puddle and darkens.

                    CORA
          Where did he --

                    LEO
          Shh.

Slowly, he points. Down the aisle. At the far door, a hand rests on the glass, dripping, fingers spread, waiting.
